WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.195
If you play games, you have seen these two bugs.

2
00:00:03.545 --> 00:00:10.713
Floors and walls covered in thin dark stripes, like a bad print. Graphics programmers call it shadow acne.

3
00:00:11.063 --> 00:00:19.020
Or the opposite. A character's shadow floats a little away from their feet, as if they were hovering. That one is called Peter Panning.

4
00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:28.309
Here are both, on the same concrete curb, the same sun, the same shadow map. The only thing that changes is one number, the bias.

5
00:00:28.659 --> 00:00:31.923
Why does one number cause both bugs? Let's find out.

6
00:00:33.123 --> 00:00:35.875
First, how does a game decide what is in shadow?

7
00:00:36.225 --> 00:00:45.206
It renders the scene once from the sun's point of view, and for each little square, called a texel, it stores how far away the closest surface is.

8
00:00:45.556 --> 00:00:55.625
That picture of distances is the shadow map. Here it is five hundred twelve texels across, and each texel covers about fifteen and a half millimetres of ground.

9
00:00:55.975 --> 00:01:02.973
Later, for every point we shade, we ask one question. Is this point farther from the sun than what the sun saw?

10
00:01:03.323 --> 00:01:07.163
If it is farther, something is in front of it, so it is in shadow.

11
00:01:08.363 --> 00:01:11.285
Now look at a tilted surface through those texels.

12
00:01:11.635 --> 00:01:17.865
Each texel stores just one depth for its whole square. So the smooth slope gets stored as a staircase.

13
00:01:18.215 --> 00:01:27.900
Points on the real surface fall behind the front edge of each step. The comparison says they are farther than what the sun saw, so they shadow themselves.

14
00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:33.498
Half of each step is wrongly dark, half is lit. Step after step, that makes stripes.

15
00:01:33.848 --> 00:01:39.949
And that is the aha. Shadow acne is the staircase of the shadow map, painted onto the surface.

16
00:01:40.299 --> 00:01:49.921
Steeper slopes make taller steps. On the flat apron, one texel spans twenty-five millimetres of depth. On the wash ramp, seventy-three and a half.

17
00:01:51.121 --> 00:01:53.254
The standard fix is called bias.

18
00:01:53.604 --> 00:01:57.401
Before comparing, slide the point a distance b toward the sun.

19
00:01:57.751 --> 00:02:03.959
In words, the point is shadowed only if its depth, minus b, is still greater than the stored depth.

20
00:02:04.309 --> 00:02:09.899
If b is taller than the step, every point clears its own staircase, and the stripes vanish.

21
00:02:10.249 --> 00:02:17.374
Notice what b really is. Not a magic tuning number, but a length, measured in millimetres along the sun ray.

22
00:02:18.574 --> 00:02:21.347
But the same slide affects real shadows too.

23
00:02:21.697 --> 00:02:29.292
Here is a steel plate, twelve millimetres thick, standing on the ground, with the sun thirty-two degrees above the horizon.

24
00:02:29.642 --> 00:02:38.154
Sliding every ground point toward the sun pulls points near the plate's foot out of its shadow. A lit gap opens between the plate and its shadow.

25
00:02:38.504 --> 00:02:47.443
For a perfect shadow map, the gap is b times the cosine of the sun's altitude, minus the plate thickness, and never less than zero.

26
00:02:47.793 --> 00:02:57.435
Second aha. Bias pays for the staircase with a length, and once that length beats the thickness of the object, its shadow detaches. That is Peter Panning.

27
00:02:58.635 --> 00:03:04.822
So we measured both, sweeping bias from zero to one hundred twenty millimetres, in steps of four.

28
00:03:05.172 --> 00:03:13.940
At zero bias, about half of all lit samples are wrongly in shadow. Point five one on the ramp, point four eight on the apron.

29
00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:19.389
The gentle apron is clean by sixteen millimetres. The steep ramp needs forty.

30
00:03:19.739 --> 00:03:27.845
Meanwhile the gap grows. Zero at the start, fifteen millimetres at thirty-six, and ninety-one millimetres at the far end.

31
00:03:28.195 --> 00:03:34.467
The strips underneath show the same thing in pictures. Stripes on the left, a floating shadow on the right.

32
00:03:35.667 --> 00:03:43.390
Where should we stop? We scored each bias by adding the remaining ramp acne and the gap, each as a fraction of its worst case.

33
00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.257
The best score lands at thirty-six millimetres.

34
00:03:46.607 --> 00:03:55.738
There, the apron is clear, a faint trace of acne remains on the ramp, about two percent, and the gap has just opened to fifteen millimetres.

35
00:03:56.088 --> 00:04:01.635
Any single constant is a compromise, because the ramp and the apron want different lengths.

36
00:04:02.835 --> 00:04:08.573
So why use one length everywhere? Give each surface a bias that matches its own step height.

37
00:04:08.923 --> 00:04:15.003
This is slope scaled bias. Steep surfaces get a long offset, gentle ones a short one.

38
00:04:15.353 --> 00:04:21.924
With a scale of one, it clears the acne on both surfaces, with a gap of only thirteen millimetres.

39
00:04:22.274 --> 00:04:27.010
The smallest constant bias that clears the ramp leaves a twenty millimetre gap.

40
00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:35.019
That is why graphics A P Is offer a slope scaled depth bias setting, and most engines expose it next to the constant one.

41
00:04:36.219 --> 00:04:37.819
Now the honest limits.

42
00:04:38.169 --> 00:04:45.209
This is one sun, one five hundred twelve square shadow map with nearest texel lookups, on a software renderer.

43
00:04:45.559 --> 00:04:55.479
No filtered soft shadows, no cascades, and no normal offset bias. The gap even rises in small plateaus, because the map is discrete.

44
00:04:55.829 --> 00:05:04.319
Every number comes from the comparison itself, never from reading the pictures. The run finishes with eleven checks passed and none failed.

45
00:05:05.519 --> 00:05:06.863
So, the takeaway.

46
00:05:07.213 --> 00:05:12.952
Shadow acne is the shadow map's staircase. Bias is a length that lifts points over it.

47
00:05:13.302 --> 00:05:20.385
Too short, and you get stripes. Too long, and shadows float away. Scaling it with the slope buys both at once.

48
00:05:20.735 --> 00:05:26.153
Shadow mapping goes back to Lance Williams, nineteen seventy-eight. Thanks for watching.
